# CSS Flexbox: Direction, Wrapping and Alignment

## 1. `flex-direction`

Controls the direction in which flex items are arranged.

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
}
```

- `row`: Horizontal arrangement; default direction.
- `column`: Vertical arrangement.
- `row-reverse`: Horizontal arrangement in reverse order.
- `column-reverse`: Vertical arrangement in reverse order.

The direction determines the main axis.

## 2. `flex-wrap`

Controls whether flex items can wrap onto additional lines.

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: nowrap;
}
```

- `nowrap`: Items remain on one flex line and may shrink if permitted by their sizing constraints.
- `wrap`: Items can move onto additional lines when necessary.
- `wrap-reverse`: Items can wrap, but the cross-axis direction of the lines is reversed.

Example:

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 16px;
}
```

If the items cannot fit on one line at their required sizes, they can continue onto another line.

**Important:** `wrap-reverse` does not simply move the last item to the top. It reverses the cross-axis direction in which flex lines are laid out. The exact visual result depends on the flex direction and available space.

## 3. `justify-content`

Controls the distribution and alignment of flex items along the main axis.

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
}
```

Common values:

- `flex-start`: Items are grouped at the main-start edge.
- `flex-end`: Items are grouped at the main-end edge.
- `center`: Items are grouped at the center.
- `space-between`: Equal space between items, with no extra distribution space at the outer edges.
- `space-around`: Equal space around each item; outer spaces are half the size of the spaces between items.
- `space-evenly`: Equal space between adjacent items and at both outer edges.

The result depends on the main axis and the available free space.

## 4. `align-items`

Controls how flex items are aligned along the cross axis within a flex line.

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
}
```

Common values:

- `flex-start`
- `flex-end`
- `center`
- `stretch`
- `baseline`

**Important:** `align-items` does not require `flex-wrap: wrap`. It works with both `nowrap` and `wrap`.

## 5. `align-content`

Controls the distribution and alignment of multiple flex lines along the cross axis.

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-content: flex-start;
}
```

Common values include:

- `flex-start`
- `flex-end`
- `center`
- `space-between`
- `space-around`
- `space-evenly`
- `stretch`

`align-content` is useful when a container has multiple flex lines and available space along the cross axis.

For example, if a container has several rows of items, `align-content: flex-start` groups those rows toward the cross-start edge.

**Important:** `align-content` is not restricted to `flex-wrap: wrap`, but its line-distribution effect is meaningful when there are multiple flex lines. With `nowrap`, there is normally only one flex line.

## 6. Parent vs. Child Properties

Most of today's properties are applied to the flex container, not directly to individual flex items.

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  align-content: flex-start;
}
```

The container controls the arrangement of its direct children.

## 7. Quick Reference

| Property | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `flex-direction` | Sets the direction of the main axis |
| `flex-wrap` | Controls whether items can wrap onto multiple lines |
| `justify-content` | Aligns or distributes items along the main axis |
| `align-items` | Aligns items along the cross axis within a line |
| `align-content` | Aligns or distributes multiple flex lines along the cross axis |
| `gap` | Sets spacing between items and flex lines |

## Key Takeaway

Understand the main axis and cross axis first. Then determine whether you are aligning items within a line or distributing multiple lines. This makes Flexbox much easier to reason about.
